{-
   A bitcast between the two readings of a sub-register width, which is where the value's bits stay
   put and the *register's* do not.

   A narrow value is held extended - unsigned zero-extended, signed sign-extended - so which
   extension a register carries is a property of the type rather than of the bits in it. A `Bitcast`
   is the one conversion that changes the type without touching the value, which makes it exactly the
   point where the required extension changes and the bits above the value do not.

   Lowering said otherwise: *"`truncateToWidth` is deliberately absent, because both sides fill the
   same register"*. Both sides do fill it, and they disagreed about what is in the part above the
   value - so natively `bitcast(207 :: U8) :: I8` answered **207** and `bitcast(-49 :: I8) :: U8`
   answered **4294967247**, against a JavaScript build that was right about both.

   **`U32`/`I32` and `U64`/`I64` were correct throughout**, which is why this survived: they fill
   their register, so there is no part above the value to disagree about. Every bitcast in `lib/` was
   at one of those widths until `Random(I8)` reinterpreted a draw, and the whole 782-fixture corpus
   moved not one golden when this was fixed.

   The globals are what keep the constant folder out of it. Folding a bitcast has always been right -
   it is the *runtime* value in a register that was wrong - so a fixture written with literals passes
   either way and asserts nothing.

   Each claim is a bit, so a wrong answer names its failing cases rather than being one number that
   differs. The widening halves are not decoration: the bug reached daylight through `Show`, which
   widens before it divides, and equality at the narrow width alone can be right while the register
   above it is not.
-}

let &byte = 207 :: U32
let &short = 40000 :: U32
let &signedByte = -49 :: I32
let &signedShort = -25536 :: I32

fn main() -> Int:
    let &score = 0 :: Int

    let toSigned8 = bitcast(truncate(byte) :: U8) :: I8
    if toSigned8 == -49 then score = score + 1
    if (widen(toSigned8) :: I32) == -49 then score = score + 2

    let toSigned16 = bitcast(truncate(short) :: U16) :: I16
    if toSigned16 == -25536 then score = score + 4
    if (widen(toSigned16) :: I32) == -25536 then score = score + 8

    let toUnsigned8 = bitcast(truncate(signedByte) :: I8) :: U8
    if toUnsigned8 == 207 then score = score + 16
    if (widen(toUnsigned8) :: U32) == 207 then score = score + 32

    let toUnsigned16 = bitcast(truncate(signedShort) :: I16) :: U16
    if toUnsigned16 == 40000 then score = score + 64
    if (widen(toUnsigned16) :: U32) == 40000 then score = score + 128

    return score
